---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import ComponentPreview from "../../components/ComponentPreview.astro";
import Used from "../../components/Used.astro";

import "../../examples/state-layer.css";
import listSource from "../../examples/state-layer.css?raw";

const usage = `
<!-- No extra imports: the state layer comes in tokens.scss.
     You just mark the item. -->
<button class="sl-tile sk-interactive" type="button">
  Interactive
</button>`;

const projectedUsage = `<!-- resulting DOM; it is written by the adapter. -->
<div
  class="sk-combobox__item sk-interactive"
  data-sk-combobox-item
  data-highlighted
  role="option"
>
  Argentina
</div>`;

const bareHtml = `<div class="sl-tiles">
  <button class="sl-tile sk-interactive" type="button">
    Interactivo
  </button>
  <button
    class="sl-tile sk-interactive"
    type="button"
    data-peek="hover"
    tabindex="-1"
  >
    Hover
  </button>
  <button
    class="sl-tile sk-interactive"
    type="button"
    data-peek="focus"
    tabindex="-1"
  >
    Focus
  </button>
  <button
    class="sl-tile sk-interactive"
    type="button"
    data-peek="pressed"
    tabindex="-1"
  >
    Pressed
  </button>
  <button
    class="sl-tile sk-interactive"
    type="button"
    aria-selected="true"
    tabindex="-1"
  >
    Selected
  </button>
  <button
    class="sl-tile sk-interactive"
    type="button"
    data-dragging="true"
    tabindex="-1"
  >
    Dragged
  </button>
  <button
    class="sl-tile sk-interactive"
    type="button"
    aria-disabled="true"
    disabled
  >
    Disabled
  </button>
</div>`;

const currentColorHtml = `<div class="sl-tiles">
  <button
    class="sl-tile sl-current-tile sk-interactive"
    type="button"
  >
    Interactivo
  </button>
  <button
    class="sl-tile sl-current-tile sk-interactive"
    type="button"
    data-peek="hover"
    tabindex="-1"
  >
    Hover
  </button>
  <button
    class="sl-tile sl-current-tile sk-interactive"
    type="button"
    data-peek="focus"
    tabindex="-1"
  >
    Focus
  </button>
  <button
    class="sl-tile sl-current-tile sk-interactive"
    type="button"
    data-peek="pressed"
    tabindex="-1"
  >
    Pressed
  </button>
  <button
    class="sl-tile sl-current-tile sk-interactive"
    type="button"
    aria-selected="true"
    tabindex="-1"
  >
    Selected
  </button>
  <button
    class="sl-tile sl-current-tile sk-interactive"
    type="button"
    data-dragging="true"
    tabindex="-1"
  >
    Dragged
  </button>
  <button
    class="sl-tile sl-current-tile sk-interactive"
    type="button"
    aria-disabled="true"
    disabled
  >
    Disabled
  </button>
</div>`;

const labHtml = `<div class="sl-lab">
  <p class="sl-lab__caption">
    Neutral surface · hover, focus, press, selected, disabled
  </p>
  <nav class="sl-list" aria-label="Folders">
    <a class="sl-item sk-interactive" href="#">
      <span data-sk-icon="info"></span>
      <span class="sl-item__label">inbox</span>
      <span class="sl-item__meta">128</span>
    </a>
    <a
      class="sl-item sk-interactive"
      href="#"
      aria-selected="true"
    >
      <span data-sk-icon="check"></span>
      <span class="sl-item__label">Enviados</span>
      <span class="sl-item__meta">12</span>
    </a>
    <a class="sl-item sk-interactive" href="#">
      <span data-sk-icon="edit"></span>
      <span class="sl-item__label">Borradores</span>
      <span class="sl-item__meta">3</span>
    </a>
    <a
      class="sl-item sk-interactive"
      href="#"
      aria-disabled="true"
    >
      <span data-sk-icon="settings"></span>
      <span class="sl-item__label">Archivados</span>
    </a>
  </nav>

  <p class="sl-lab__caption">
    Saturated fill · the layer is currentColor (on-accent)
  </p>
  <div class="sl-actions">
    <button
      class="sk-button sk-interactive"
      data-sk-button
      data-variant="primary"
      type="button"
    >
      Publicar
    </button>
    <button
      class="sk-button sk-interactive"
      data-sk-button
      type="button"
    >
      Cancel
    </button>
    <button
      class="sk-button sk-interactive"
      data-sk-button
      data-variant="ghost"
      type="button"
      disabled
    >
      Deshacer
    </button>
  </div>
</div>`;

const ladder = [
  ["disabled", "--state-layer-disabled-opacity", "force layer to 0"],
  ["dragged", "--state-layer-dragged-opacity", ""],
  ["pressed", "--state-layer-pressed-opacity", ""],
  ["focus", "--state-layer-focus-opacity", ""],
  ["hover", "--state-layer-hover-opacity", ""],
  ["selected", "--state-layer-selected-opacity", "the base step"],
];
---

<Base
  title="State layer"
  description="Two ways to connect the same feedback: direct DOM states and machine-projected states."
>
  <h1>State layer</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    The same semi-transparent overlay expresses hover, focus, pressed, selected and dragged. The system has two ways to connect it: from the real state of the DOM or from the logical state of a machine. They share paint and tokens; They are not two visual variants nor two superimposed layers.
  </p>

  <h2>Two connection types, single layer</h2>
  <p>
    The type <strong>describes who knows the state</strong>. If the element itself receives hover, focus and press, the connection is direct. If the DOM focus remains on another node and a machine knows which item is active, the adapter projects that state to the DOM.
  </p>
  <div class="sk-table-scroll">
    <table class="sk-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Tipo</th><th>Source of truth</th><th>Use it when</th><th>Who connects it</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Directo</strong></td>
          <td><code>:hover</code>, <code>:focus-visible</code>, <code>:active</code>, ARIA y <code>:has()</code></td>
          <td>The same button, link, tile or wrapper owns the interaction.</td>
          <td>The consumer adds <code>sk-interactive</code> once.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Proyectado</strong></td>
          <td><code>data-highlighted</code>, <code>data-selected</code>, <code>data-disabled</code> and other machine states.</td>
          <td>A composite widget retains focus on its input or root, but activates an internal item.</td>
          <td>The adapter and CSS of the component; the consumer does not recreate the mapping.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>1. Direct: the element has the state</h2>
  <p>
    Button, link and tile receive the browser states in the same node that is painted. The pattern comes in <code>tokens.scss</code>: the consumer only adds <code>sk-interactive</code>. Its selectors also read ARIA and use <code>: has()</code> when a wrapper contains the native input. The <code>:: before</code> remains inert in everything else.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={usage} label="HTML · direct connection" note="mark the target once" />

  <h3>All states, two surfaces</h3>
  <p>
    The two previews freeze the same entire staircase for comparison without moving the pointer. The first uses a neutral surface; the second, an inverse surface whose layer inherits <code>currentColor</code>. In both, the first tile is still interactive.
  </p>
  <ComponentPreview html={bareHtml} label="Neutral surface · all states" />
  <ComponentPreview
    html={currentColorHtml}
    label="currentColor · all states"
  />
  <p>
    Selected, hover, focus, pressed, and dragged now advance in visible steps. In high contrast the wash does not increase: doing so would bring the background closer to the text and dull the surface. Instead, a keyline inset appears whose thickness grows with each state.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Projected: the machine has state</h2>
  <p>
    In Combobox, Menu, DatePicker and TreeView the focus can remain on the input or the root while the arrows activate another item. That item does not necessarily match <code>: focus-visible</code>. The adapter writes <code>data-highlighted</code>, <code>data-selected</code>, or <code>data-disabled</code>, and the component's CSS translates that logical state into the same opacity tokens.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    code={projectedUsage}
    label="Resulting DOM · projected connection"
    note="it is written by the adapter; not copied"
  />
  <div class="sk-alert" data-tone="info" data-presentation="accent">
    <div class="sk-alert__content">
      <p>
        <strong>The consumer does not choose between two classes. </strong> Use <code>sk-interactive</code> for a DOM target of your own. When consuming a composite widget, its adapter and stylesheet already have the projected connection.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Same layer, different surfaces</h2>
  <p>
    Neutral surface and saturated fill are not additional types: they only change the <code>currentColor</code> that colors the same layer. The list uses the text color; on the primary button the veil is <code>on-accent</code>. Change brand, mode or contrast: the contract is maintained.
  </p>
  <ComponentPreview html={labHtml} label="Lista + acciones" />

  <h2>The tint is <code>currentColor</code></h2>
  <p>
    The color of the layer is, the content color of the component itself. A single token covers all cases:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>It subsumes black-on-light and white-on-dark:</strong> A neutral surface has dark text in light mode and light text in dark mode, and the layer alone darkens or lightens it without a branch per mode.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>And is correct over saturated fills</strong>, where setting black or white fails: the content of a primary button uses <code>on-accent</code>, which resolves based on the fill. The layer inherits that exact tone instead of assuming one.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Zero color tokens per component. </strong> One fits all.</li>
  </ul>


  <h2>Exactly one active layer</h2>
  <p>
    The two connections write a single opacity over a single pseudo-element; They never stack overlays. On direct connection, the shared pattern resolves concurrent matches to this ladder, from highest to lowest priority:
  </p>
  <div class="sk-table-scroll">
    <table class="sk-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Prioridad</th><th>Status</th><th>Opacidad usada</th><th></th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          ladder.map(([state, token, note], i) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="count">{i + 1}</td>
              <td class="mono">{state}</td>
              <td><Used token={token} /></td>
              <td class="count">{note}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="sk-alert" data-tone="info" data-presentation="accent">
    <div class="sk-alert__content">
    <p>
      <strong>The ladder does not use combined selectors.</strong> Each state rule assigns the
      <em>same</em> <code>--state-layer-opacity</code>, and all selectors have the same
      specificity (0,2,0), so when several match, it decides the <strong>order in the
      file</strong>. The rules are written in ascending priority: last one wins.
    </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>In the direct connection, <code>selected</code> is the lowest rung</h2>
  <p>
    A persistent base beneath hover would create two overlapping layers and therefore combined
    opacity, which the model forbids. Selected is the lowest state; hover and pressed replace it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The selection never disappears because <strong>always carries an independent indicator</strong>: a check, a filled control, a rail, a type weight. In the list above, "Sent" has an accent rail and emphasized typography, it does not depend on the layer. If you need a stronger selected+hover, you define a combined opacity token; never a sum in runtime.
  </p>

  <h2>Accessibility, non-negotiable</h2>
  <p>The layer <strong>is never the only signal</strong>:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      The focus carries an independent ring ( de ), verified at ≥3:1 by the validator. That is why it lives in its own <code>--focus-ring-*</code> family and not within <code>--state-layer-*</code>: the name carries the guarantee.
    </li>
    <li>The selection carries a real indicator.</li>
    <li>
      <code>disabled</code> dims with component tokens and <strong>forces the layer to 0</strong>.
    </li>
    <li><code>: focus-visible</code> is used, not <code>: focus</code>, so a mouse click does not leave a layer stuck.</li>
    <li>The fade respects <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>High contrast adds a keyline, no more wash</h2>
  <p>
    Light and dark share the same scale: <code>currentColor</code> already changes with the mode. High contrast also does not duplicate opacities. That would bring the background closer to the text color and make the control read off. <code>[data-contrast="high"]</code> preserves the wash, adds a state-proportional keyline inset, and increases the focus ring to <code>3px</code>. Activate high contrast above and compare both galleries.
  </p>

  <h2>Your CSS</h2>
  <p>
    For your own DOM target, the pattern sends the structure of the layer and the ladder; you write the layout and add the class. Composite widgets already send their mapping projected in the CSS of the component:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={listSource} label="src/examples/state-layer.css" note="the CSS of the previews ↞" />

  <p>
    Zero state colors and zero hardcoded opacities. Use direct connection on your own controls; for machine states, it consumes the component instead of duplicating its internal selectors.
  </p>

  <div class="sk-alert" data-tone="info" data-presentation="accent">
    <div class="sk-alert__content">
    <p>
      A consumer with a more specific selector than <code>.sk-interactive: hover</code> can break the ladder. It is a CSS limitation, the same one that <a href="/en/styling-hooks">accepts in the</a> styling hooks contract.
    </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
